  "client_view_text": " Here\u2019s a partial example:\n```\n// Initial state\nOp 1: Insert {1, \u201cAlice\u201d}\nOp Here's a write-ahead log: What is a write-ahead log, and how does it help with database consistency and failure recovery?\nA write-ahead log, also known as a redo log or WAL, is a mechanism used by some database management systems (DBMS) to ensure database consistency and facilitate recovery after a crash. When a transaction is prepared to commit, the DBMS writes a record of the transaction to the log before applying it to the database. This ensures that any transaction records are safely stored before they affect the database.\nIf a crash occurs during this process, the DBMS can recover the database to a consistent state by replaying the transactions from the log. This ensures that any transactions that were in progress at the time of the crash are either fully applied or rolled back, maintaining the database's consistency.\nThe write-ahead log allows the DBMS to recover from a crash by following these steps: 1) Apply all transactions from the log that have already been committed, 2) Roll back any transactions that were in progress and had not been committed, and 3) Reapply any transactions that were partially applied but not yet committed. This ensures that the database is brought back to a consistent state, and the DBMS can continue operating without compromising data integrity. By"
